              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#introduction_and_motivation Introduction and motivation
          %p
            Urban legend
            %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_general_relativity#Sir_Arthur_Eddington"} has it
            that there was a time when only three people understood
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein"} Einstein
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            – “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"}> general relativity
            ”.
          %p
            Whether true or not, one of the three was
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Hilbert"} David Hilbert
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
          %p
            classical Einstein gravity is simply the study of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/critical+points"} critical points
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral"} integral
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+curvature"} scalar curvature
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/density"} density
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} functional
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+metrics"} pseudo-Riemannian metrics
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
          %p
            By trusting that a fundamental theory of physics should have a fundamental formulation in mathematics,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert"} Hilbert
            was able to essentially scoop Einstein (see
            %a{:href => "Einstein-Hilbert+action#History"} here
            for the history), that’s why this functional is now called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Hilbert+action+functional"} Einstein-Hilbert action functional
          %p
            Hilbert had promoted this general idea before as part of the famous eponymous
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert%27s+problems"} Hilbert's problems
              in mathematics, from 1900. Here
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert%27s+6th+problem"} Hilbert's 6th problem
              asks
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematicians
              generally to find
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
              for
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theories
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
          %p Since then a list of such axiomatizations has been found, for instance
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mechanics"} mechanics
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+geometry"} Riemannian geometry
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Weil+theory"} Chern-Weil theory
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+algebra"} operator algebra
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TFT"} topological
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+quantum+field+theory"} local quantum field theory
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category"} monoidal (∞,n)-category
                  theory
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ⋮
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdots
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ⋮
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdots
          %p Two aspects of this list are noteworthy: on the one hand, it contains crown jewels of mathematics, on the other the items appear unrelated and piecemeal.
          %p
            As a student,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"} William Lawvere
            was exposed to the proposal to axiomatize
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thermodynamics"} thermodynamics
            as what was called “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+thermodynamics"}> rational thermodynamics
            ”. He realized that a fundamental
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundation"} foundation
            of such
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum+physics"} continuum physics
            first of all requires a good foundation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            itself. Looking over his life publication record (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"} here
          %p
            %strong Plan.
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                lay the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
                (“
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"}> ETCS
                ”)
            %li
              %p
                lay the foundations of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"}> synthetic differential geometry
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesion
            %li
              %p
                lay the foundations of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum+physics"} continuum physics
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes+of+laws+of+motion"}> toposes of laws of motion
                ).
          %p
            Lawvere became famous for his groundbreaking contributions to the first two items (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"}> categorical logic
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} elementary topos theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theories"} algebraic theories
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} SDG
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "William%20Lawvere#MotivationFromFoundationsOfPhysics"} list of quotations here
          %p Grandiose as this plan is, we have to note that in the above form it falls short in each item, by modern standards:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                modern
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
                is naturally founded not in
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-topos+theory"} higher topos theory
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            %li
              %p
                modern
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
                is not just about “variable sets” (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves"}> sheaves
                ) but is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
                about “variable
                = succeed "\u201D," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
                = succeed "\u201D," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+homotopy+types"} geometric homotopy types
                = succeed "\u201D;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-stack"} higher stacks
            %li
              %p
                modern
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
                goes beyond
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum+physics"} continuum physics
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
                (small distance) classical physics is refined by
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
          %p
            Therefore what is needed is a foundation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/high+energy+physics"} high energy physics
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
            formulated in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+topos+theory"} higher topos theory
          %p
            In the following we illustrate three aspects of such a refined theory, following (
            %a{:href => "#dcct"}> dcct
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#syntheticQFT"} sythQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
          %h2#HigherMappingSpaces
            %strong I)
            Mapping spaces in gauge theory and General covariance
          %p
            One basic point emphasized in (
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere67"}> Lawvere 67
            ) is that central to the formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            is the existence of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+spaces"} mapping spaces
            which satisfy the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+law"} exponential law
          %p
            (notice:
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+space"} mapping space
            … space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trajectories"} trajectories
            …
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
          %p
            Indeed, generically a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+system"} physical system
            is specified by
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                called
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                or
                = succeed ";" do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                called
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
                or
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
                or
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
                    of
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
          %p
            such that a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trajectory"} trajectory
            or
            %em history
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+field"} field configurations
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/map"} map
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Sigma \longrightarrow X
                  \\,.
          %p
            For instance for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma = \mathbb{R}
            the abstract
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \to X
            may be taken to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trajectory"} trajectory
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
          %p
            (Notice that after
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+quantization"} second quantization
            the roles change. First the domain
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            and the codomain
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"}> sigma-model
            )”, then after second quantization spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            becomes the domain (hence becomes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            in the above).)
          %p
            Hence the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+space"} mapping space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Sigma,X]
            is the space of all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trajectories"} trajectories
            (the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+space"} path space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain"} domain
            of the infamous
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
          %p
            Lawvere observed that this not only needs to exist as a decent “space”, it also needs to satisfy the axiom of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Sigma \coloneqq \mathbb{R} \times \Sigma_{d-1}
          %p
            into
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time"} time
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{R} \times \Sigma_{d-1} \longrightarrow X
          %p are equivalently trajectories of fields on space
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{R}  \longrightarrow [\Sigma_{d-1}, X]
          %p and also equivalently a collection of field trajectories for each point of space
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Sigma_{d-1}  \longrightarrow [\mathbb{R}, X]
                  \\,.
          %p
            This led Lawvere to recognize that physics (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"}> prequantum physics
            , to be precise) is to be formulated in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SmthMfd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SmthMfd
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            is too small to accomplish this. But the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sheaves"} category of sheaves
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi SmthMdf
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(SmthMdf)
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            of smooth manifolds is the canonical improvement. Objects in here include
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+spaces"} diffeological spaces
            and general
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+spaces"} smooth spaces
          %p
            Better still, there is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sheaves"} category of sheaves
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi FSmthMfd
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(FSmthMfd)
            on the site of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+smooth+manifolds"} formal smooth manifolds
            – known as the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cahiers+topos"} Cahiers topos
            \. This also contains
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+objects"} infinitesimal objects
            and indeed interprets the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
          %p
            But actually in modern physics one needs a bit more than this. Physics is fundamentally governed by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+equivalence"} gauge equivalence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            configurations are equal, we must ask if they are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalent
          %p
            A fundamental example of this is
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein"} Einstein
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+covariance"} general covariance
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi U
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  s \;\colon\; U \hookrightarrow \Sigma
          %p
            is a region in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Σ
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo ≃
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \colon \Sigma \stackrel{\simeq}{\longrightarrow} \Sigma
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphism
            acting on spacetime, then the “translated region”
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo *
                  %mi s
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi U
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi Σ
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo ≃
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\phi^\ast s \;\colon\; U \hookrightarrow \Sigma \stackrel{\simeq}{\longrightarrow} \Sigma
          %p
            is “the same”, for all physical purposes. Stated this way in ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            this is confusing, and historically it was confusing: this confusion is essentially what is known as the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hole+paradox"}> hole paradox
            ”.
          %p
            This apparent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paradox"} paradox
            is resolved in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+topos+theory"} higher topos theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            has internal symmetries, it is a
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
          %p
            For
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+covariance"} general covariance
            this means by the above that spacetime is not actually the spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoid"} action groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+stack"} quotient stack
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi Diff
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Sigma//Diff(\Sigma)
          %p
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            by its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism+group"} diffeomorphism group
            (regarded as a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+group"} diffeological group
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi Diff
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma//Diff(\Sigma)
            is the thing which is such that maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi Diff
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \longrightarrow \Sigma//Diff(\Sigma)
            into it behave just as they should as demanded by general covariance.
          %p
            This is the formalization of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+covariance"}> general covariance
            ” for regions
            %em inside
            spacetime. The other thing now is general covariance for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            %em on
            spacetime. It turns out that the formalism automatically handles these now:
          %p
            For notice that the above means now that we also need to consider the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+spaces"} mapping spaces
            refined to higher topos theory. A fundamental fact is that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Grp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \in Grp(\mathbf{H})
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+object+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} group object
            then the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} higher slice topos
            over its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            is equivalently the collection of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-action"} G-actions
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mi G
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi G
                  %mi Act
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{H}_{/\mathbf{B}G} \simeq G Act(\mathbf{H})
                  \\,.
          %p
            Under this equivalence a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-action"} higher action
            is identified with the universal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+infinity-bundle"} associated bundle
            which it induces
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Σ
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mi Σ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mi Diff
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi Σ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi Diff
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi Σ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∈
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mi Diff
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Diff
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Act
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\left(
                  \\array{
                  \\Sigma &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; \Sigma//Diff(\Sigma)
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \mathbf{B}Diff(\Sigma)
                  }
                  \\right)
                  \\;\;
                  \\in
                  \\;\;
                  \\mathbf{H}_{/\mathbf{B}Diff(\Sigma)}
                  \\simeq Diff(\Sigma)Act(\mathbf{H})
                  \\,.
          %p
            Lawvere also introduced
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
            and understood
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product"} dependent product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum"} dependent sum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum
            as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-topos+theory"} higher topos theory
            this becomes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
            as follows:
          %div
            %p
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant cohomology
                in terms of
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos+theory"} (∞,1)-topos theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
              (
