                          %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp;  \int_{dR} \Omega A &amp;&amp; \longrightarrow &amp;&amp; \flat_{dR}\Sigma A
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \nearrow &amp; &amp; \searrow &amp; &amp; \nearrow_{\mathrlap{\theta_A}} &amp;&amp; \searrow
                  \\\
                  \\flat \int_{dR} \Omega A  &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; A &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; \int \flat_{dR}\Sigma A
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \searrow &amp;  &amp; \nearrow &amp; &amp; \searrow &amp;&amp; \nearrow_{\mathrlap{\int \theta_A}}
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \flat A &amp;&amp; \longrightarrow &amp;&amp; \int A
                  }
                  \\,,
          %p
            is guaranteed to consist of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullback"} homotopy pullback
            squares, by the nature of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triples"} adjoint triples
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modalities"} modalities
            (see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+cohesion"} tangent cohesion
            for more on this).
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#BunkeNikolausVoelkl13"}> Bunke-Nikolaus-Völkl 13
            ) it is shown that this is universally the “differential cohomology diagram” which hence exhibits
            %em every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+type"} stable homotopy type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} cohesive homotopy type theory
            as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology+theory"} differential cohomology theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            for abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+fields"} higher gauge fields
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} cohesive homotopy type theory
            is a universal ambient context for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            and hence for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+fields"} higher gauge fields
            appearing in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            – whence the title “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/differential+cohomology+in+a+cohesive+topos"}> differential cohomology in a cohesive topos
            ”.
          %p
            Using this and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted cohomology
            available in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+cohesion"} tangent cohesion
            (
            %a{:href => "#BunkeNikolaus"}> Bunke-Nikolaus
            ) show the existence of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+differential+K-theory"} twisted differential K-theory
          %h2#references References
